Letting a worker fail is useful when recovery returns it to a known state. Preserve indispensable recovery data; rebuild disposable state.

Book basis: [B1](/lore/durability-bibliography#b1-erlang-and-otp-in-action), [B2](/lore/durability-bibliography#b2-elixir-in-action), [B3](/lore/durability-bibliography#b3-designing-elixir-systems-with-otp).

## Failure it prevents

A fresh worker reuses inconsistent state or forgets critical coordination data.

## Our Effect 4.0.0 reading

`Activity` results and `WorkflowEngine` can support reconstruction with persistent storage. A new [fiber](/lore/concurrent-work-needs-an-owner) does not restore old local variables or [machine](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines) memory.

Source: `packages/effect/src/workflow/Activity.ts`, `packages/effect/src/workflow/WorkflowEngine.ts`, `packages/effect/src/Fiber.ts`.

## How to check

Recover from an incomplete step using durable authority. Check behavior rather than merely checking that the worker is running. This check is our proposal. The books do not prescribe it.

Continue with [Replay needs stable inputs](/lore/replay-needs-stable-inputs), [Durable timers need recovery](/lore/durable-timers-need-recovery).

Connect this to [the existing rat-stack guidance](/lore/lifecycles-are-machines).

